<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>

  <!--
    This file exists so that a restore resolves the same packages on every machine, and so that
    Visual Studio can open the solution without errors.

    Without it, NuGet falls back to the machine-wide configuration. As soon as that lists more than
    one remote feed — nuget.org plus an internal Azure Artifacts, Nexus or ProGet feed, which is the
    normal state of a developer machine at a bank — central package management refuses to guess
    between unmapped sources and restore reports NU1507 on every project:

        There are 2 package sources defined in your configuration. When using central package
        management, please map your package sources with package source mapping or specify a single
        package source.

    Because Directory.Build.props sets TreatWarningsAsErrors, that warning is an error, and the
    solution appears to fail for reasons that have nothing to do with the code. CI never saw it
    because the build agents have exactly one source configured.

    Note that the check counts *remote* sources: a local folder source, such as the "Microsoft Visual
    Studio Offline Packages" entry a stock VS install adds, does not trigger it on its own. This was
    verified by reproducing NU1507 against two HTTP feeds and confirming a folder source did not.

    <clear /> is what fixes it: inherited sources are discarded, one source remains, and NU1507
    cannot fire regardless of what the machine has configured.
  -->

  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" />
  </packageSources>

  <!--
    Package source mapping is not needed to silence NU1507 once <clear /> leaves a single source, but
    it is kept deliberately.

    It states that every package must come from nuget.org, which is the documented defence against
    dependency-confusion substitution: a package published to another feed under one of these names
    cannot be picked up simply because it carries a higher version. That matters more here than in
    most repositories, because this library is consumed by banking software.

    Adding an internal feed belongs here — declare the feed above, then add a <packageSource> block
    listing the prefixes it owns (for example <package pattern="YourBank.*" />). Leaving the mapping
    off instead would restore the substitution risk, so please extend it rather than remove it.
  -->
  <packageSourceMapping>
    <packageSource key="nuget.org">
      <package pattern="*" />
    </packageSource>
  </packageSourceMapping>

</configuration>
